If your Selenium tests pass individually but fail when you run the entire suite, the issue is usually one of these:

1. Shared WebDriver Instance

A test leaves the browser in an unexpected state for the next test.

Bad

Java
WebDriver driver;
 
@BeforeAll
static void setup() {
driver = new ChromeDriver();
}
 

Better

Java
WebDriver driver;
 
@BeforeEach
void setup() {
driver = new ChromeDriver();
}
 
@AfterEach
void tearDown() {
driver.quit();
}
2. Tests Depend on Execution Order

One test creates data that another test expects.

Bad

Java
@Test
void login_test() { ... }
 
@Test
void search_test() {
// assumes login_test already ran
}

Each test should be independent.

3. Browser State Not Reset

Cookies, local storage, session storage, or authentication persists between tests.

Add cleanup:

Java
@AfterEach
void tearDown() {
driver.manage().deleteAllCookies();
driver.quit();
}
4. Parallel Execution

JUnit or TestNG may be running tests simultaneously.

Check:

JUnit

.properties
junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled=false

TestNG

XML
<suite parallel="false">
5. Timing Issues

A page loads slower when multiple tests run.

Replace:

Java
Thread.sleep(2000);

with explicit waits:

Java
WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
 
WebElement element = wait.until(
ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(
By.id("accept")
)
);
6. Static Variables

A common cause that is easy to miss:

Java
static WebDriver driver;
static String userName;

Static state is shared across all tests and can cause interference.

7. Test Data Collisions

All tests use the same record:

Java
user = "testuser";

When running together, one test modifies or deletes data needed by another.

Use unique data:

Java
String user = "testuser_" + System.currentTimeMillis();

To diagnose further, please paste:

the full error message from the Run All Tests execution,
your @BeforeEach, @AfterEach, @BeforeAll, and @AfterAll methods,
whether you're using JUnit 5 or TestNG.

The stack trace will usually reveal the exact cause within a few lines.